  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Consider the function $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-6x+9.$ Given that ${f}^{\u2032}(1)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{\u2033}(1)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=1$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=1$",
  "worked_solution": "We're given that $x=1$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-6x+9,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)=6x-6.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2033}(x)=6.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2033}(1) & =6>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is positive, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
